From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: blurbery <271863866+blurbery@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] capi: return an error instead of aborting on any input

libdovi's C library is built with -Cpanic=abort, so any panic while
parsing, converting or writing an RPU ends the whole process, and FFmpeg
and libplacebo hand it RPUs straight from the media. Malformed RPUs reach
several: a mapping method code of 2 or more and unknown CM v4.0 level 8,
9 and 10 block lengths hit unreachable!(), linear interpolation in a
first-order piece hits unimplemented!(), an RPU of valid start bytes
followed by zeros underflows the CRC32 range, and a curve that mixes
polynomial and MMR pieces parses but panics in the writer. Very large
Exp-Golomb counts are worse: they size allocations up front (pivots,
curve pieces, extension blocks, the AV1 EMDF payload), and an allocation
the system refuses aborts whatever the panic strategy.

Build the C library with -Cpanic=unwind, and run the body of every
exported function inside catch_unwind, so no panic reaches or aborts at
the FFI boundary. A caught panic gives the function's normal error
result: the parsers return an RpuOpaque (or RpuOpaqueList) carrying an
error, the getters and writers return a null pointer and the writers
record the error, and the functions returning a status return -1 and
record it. A function that was changing the RPU when it panicked
(conversion, active area offsets, mapping removal) also drops the RPU,
so a half-converted RPU is never written out. A null buffer, or a length
no slice can have, is a parse error.

Check every count read from the RPU against what the rest of the input
could hold before reserving anything for it, and fail the parse with a
single-line error when it cannot: a pivot takes the base layer bit
depth, a piece at least two bits, an extension block at least nine and
an EMDF payload byte eight, so these checks only fire on input that
could never parse. Reserve at most 64 elements up front even then; the
vectors still grow as elements are parsed.

Refuse to write an RPU that holds u64::MAX in a field parsing only
stores (an Exp-Golomb code with 64 leading zeros gives that value).
bitvec_helpers' write_ue cannot encode it: its length computation wraps,
so it writes a run of 4294967295 zero bits (512 MiB) before failing. The
write already failed; it now fails with a single-line error before
reserving anything.

The header query returns null when the header says display management
data is present but the display management query could not build it,
because libplacebo reads that result without a null check.

Freeing an RpuOpaqueList that carries an error no longer builds a Vec
from a null pointer.

Input that did not abort before gives the same results, bytes and
structures, and the same error texts except where a check above fires
first. The header and the exported symbols are unchanged. Applies to
dovi_tool tag libdovi-3.3.2 (dolby_vision 3.3.2).

